WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.283
Excitatory cortical neurons are born in zones at the bottom of the wall, then climb.

2
00:00:09.223 --> 00:00:11.723
So how can you tell, weeks later, when one was born?

3
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:15.773
You date its last S phase, when DNA is copied.

4
00:00:16.593 --> 00:00:19.933
That is the final S phase of the cell that divides to make it.

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:26.013
Give a pregnant mouse one dose of thymidine carrying tritium, a radioactive form of

6
00:00:26.013 --> 00:00:27.013
hydrogen.

7
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.263
Only DNA uses thymidine.

8
00:00:30.123 --> 00:00:33.663
So only cells copying DNA in the next hour or so are marked.

9
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:37.693
The labeled cell still divides once to make a neuron.

10
00:00:38.613 --> 00:00:41.033
Each extra division after that halves the label.

11
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:48.337
A neuron still heavily labeled weeks later made its last DNA copy while the label

12
00:00:48.337 --> 00:00:50.757
was there. The pulse dates its birth.

13
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.370
The label marks that S phase.

14
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.790
It does not tell the cell anything.

15
00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:02.870
Now two models. In model one, each new neuron is added at the bottom and shoves the

16
00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:03.870
older ones outward.

17
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.850
In model two, each new neuron climbs past the older ones and settles above them.

18
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:17.760
Predict. Pulse on embryonic day 12 then look at postnatal day 21 Where does each

19
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.580
model put the heavily labeled cells?

20
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:30.650
Model one puts them nearest the surface, shoved up by everything added beneath them.

21
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:32.790
Model two puts them deepest.

22
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:39.930
In 1961, Jay Angevine and Richard Sidman gave pulses on different embryonic days.

23
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.633
Cells labeled earliest lay deepest, beside the white matter.

24
00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:48.513
Cells labeled later lay progressively nearer the surface.

25
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:52.107
Later cells had passed earlier ones.

26
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:55.767
It is a strong tendency, with overlap between days.

27
00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:02.057
Here is the signature evidence, idealized.

28
00:02:02.057 --> 00:02:08.057
Heavily labeled cells from an E12 pulse sit mostly in layers 5 and 6.

29
00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:13.507
From an E16 pulse, they sit mostly in layers 2 and 3.

30
00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.527
Lightly labeled cells divided again after the pulse.

31
00:02:18.447 --> 00:02:21.527
Born later, at many times, they scatter through many layers.

32
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:28.440
In 1974, Pasko Rakic found the same order in the visual cortex of rhesus monkeys.

33
00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.730
In the mouse, layers 2 to 6 are made over about six embryonic days.

34
00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.710
Subplate and layer 6 come first, upper layers last.

35
00:02:38.983 --> 00:02:43.783
Then each later wave passes the neurons already in the cortical plate and settles

36
00:02:43.783 --> 00:02:44.783
above them.

37
00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:47.906
Notice the exception.

38
00:02:48.866 --> 00:02:52.906
The preplate cells are born first, yet they end up both at the top and at the

39
00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:56.726
bottom. Earlier means deeper only inside the cortical plate.

40
00:02:57.716 --> 00:02:59.596
This is a rule of the neocortex.

41
00:03:00.536 --> 00:03:04.336
The retina uses birth order too, but builds its layers another way.

42
00:03:06.416 --> 00:03:11.436
The takeaway: a thymidine pulse dates a birthday, and later-born neurons pass

43
00:03:11.436 --> 00:03:13.276
earlier ones to settle above them.
